# Recorded source payloads are evidence: never line-ending-converted, on any
# platform, so a fixture stays byte-identical to what the provider sent.
tests/fixtures/** -text

# An executed notebook is the analysis of record, and re-running it rewrites the
# whole file. Pinning it to LF keeps that diff to the cells whose outputs really
# changed, instead of to every line of JSON a Windows tool just re-wrote with
# CRLF -- which is how a corrupted markdown cell hides in plain sight.
# See https://github.com/cweber12/kelp-compare/issues/83
*.ipynb text eol=lf

# Rendered figures are binary evidence of a run, and a text filter on Windows
# would corrupt them exactly as it corrupts a notebook. Pin them, for the same
# reason the rule above pins fixtures.
notebooks/figures/*.png binary
